            {"id": "b", "text": "No performance difference; modern CPUs aggregate channels transparently", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. CPUs do not aggregate channels transparently in terms of bandwidth. Single-channel access is a 64-bit wide bus; dual-channel doubles it to 128-bit. The performance difference is measurable, especially in integrated-GPU systems and memory-intensive applications."},
            {"id": "c", "text": "The system will not POST because unmatched slot population is unsupported", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Most motherboards support single-DIMM configurations and will POST and run normally; they simply operate in single-channel mode. Only certain server platforms with strict population rules require populated matching slots to boot."},
            {"id": "d", "text": "Quad-channel operation is activated when any slot is populated", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Quad-channel requires four DIMMs (one per channel) on platforms that support it (e.g., Intel LGA2066/LGA4189). Consumer platforms with two channels can only achieve dual-channel at best, and only with the correct slot population."},
