            {"id": "b", "text": "15K RPM drives use a wider data bus, transferring more bits per revolution", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. HDD data bus width (SATA vs SAS interface) is independent of RPM. The data transfer rate per revolution (linear density) is a different parameter; RPM primarily affects how quickly sectors rotate into position (latency), not bus width."},
            {"id": "c", "text": "15K RPM drives have larger cache buffers, allowing more data to be prefetched", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. While enterprise 15K RPM drives often have larger cache (e.g., 64–256 MB) than consumer 5,400 RPM drives, cache size alone does not explain the random I/O advantage. The fundamental mechanical advantage is rotational speed and seek time."},
            {"id": "d", "text": "5,400 RPM drives cannot interface with SAS backplanes, making them incompatible", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. While incompatibility is a real practical concern, this answer does not explain the performance metric. The question asks about the metric explaining performance, not interface compatibility."},
